Test deck 2 for SMARDDA-PFC
---------------------------
Deck assumes that SMARDDA-PFC is installed in the directory
indicated by the shell environment variable $SMITER_DIR
and that this test-deck directory is $HSAUX2. These might be set as
export SMITER_DIR=~/smardda/pfc
export HSAUX2=~/smardda/smiteraux2
(If these environment variables are unset, tests.bash will make a guess
as to where smardda-pfc and the test-deck have been installed.)

The serial test-deck should be invoked using
./tests.bash
which should take less than 15 minutes on a high-end laptop or desktop. 
More test-deck details and graphics for comparison with test output are provided
by browser-based documentation, see
$SMITER_DIR/doc/srcdoc/html/index.html
Local directory doc is under development.
When no longer needed, the test-deck outputs can be removed using
./tests_allclean.bash
OR to keep the _powx.vtk files, use
./tests_clean.bash

Set up for testing MPI parallelism is also described in 
$SMITER_DIR/doc/srcdoc/html/index.html
and the test-deck MPI scripts invoked using
./test_mpi_ITER-valid.bash
./test_mpi_JET-radn.bash

The test cases occupy separate directories
DEMO-double-null
ITER-valid
JET-ant
JET-radn

Most of the data for the test-deck are in subdirectory Data,
which in turn has subdirectories
Equilibrium - .eqdsk files
Geometry - contains .dat files, .bash scripts and .ctl files for datvtk to generate geometry from points,
either directly or invoked by the scripts
(After test-deck execution, also contains log files from datvtk, and .vtk files produced by vtktfm)
Misc - radiation source files, and files for production of gnuplot graphics
Physics-Radiation - radiation data files derived using ADAS
VTK - contains .vtk files used by the test-deck, antenna file produced using ParaView script, plus
vtktfm ctl files for transforming and combining vtk files from CAD and/or datvtk.
(After test-deck execution, also contains vtktfm log files.)

Each test case is executed using a separate file case.bash using .ctl files
stored in the case-specific directory in the appropriate subdirectories -
G for target geometry processing,  P for power deposition calculation etc.
The presence of case-specific skeleton files RES.ctl and SHAD.ctl provides
a potentially useful degree of flexibility, facilitating study of different geometries
and equilibria.

The conventions applying to the outputs are listed
in the online SMARDDA-PFC documentation.

